course: physics
lesson: 7
title: Living with friction: oil, wheels and grip
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: People spend a lot of cleverness making friction smaller where it gets in the way and bigger where they need grip.
explain: To make it smaller, we put oil between rubbing parts, or we use wheels and ball bearings so that things roll instead of rub.
explain: To make it bigger, we use rubber, chalk and rough surfaces: try opening a stuck jar with a rubber glove and it turns at once.
explain: Without any friction at all, you could not walk, hold a pencil or even tie your shoes, because everything would slide off everything else.
fact: Oil on a squeaky hinge makes a slippery layer between the metal parts, so they slide instead of rubbing.
fact: Car engines need oil; without it their metal parts would rub, heat up and seize (jam solid).
fact: Wheels roll instead of rub, so a suitcase on wheels moves easily, and before wheels people rolled huge stones on logs.
fact: Ball bearings are tiny steel balls that roll between a wheel and its axle, and they are in skateboards, bikes, fans and washing machines.
fact: Climbers and gymnasts put chalk on their hands because it soaks up sweat, and dry skin grips better than sweaty skin.
fact: A rubber glove helps open a jar because soft rubber moulds into the tiny bumps on the lid and grips it.
fact: Running a metal jar lid under hot water also helps, because the metal grows a little.
fact: Two books with their pages interleaved (slid in between each other, one by one) are almost impossible to pull apart, because the small grips of hundreds of pages add up.
fact: On a TV show, two interleaved phone books could not be pulled apart even by two cars.
fact: A spacecraft coming home glows red hot mostly because the air in front is squeezed and heats up, so it needs a heat shield.
fact: Fish are slippery because their skin is covered in slime, which also protects them from germs, so wet your hands before holding one.
fact: Once a tyre slides, it grips less than when it rolls, which is why a spinning tyre screeches and good drivers start smoothly.
fact: A ruler on a tilted desk starts to slide only when the slope is steep enough for gravity to beat friction, and a rubber eraser needs a much steeper slope.
fact: Knots in laces hold only because the lace rubs on itself.
fact: A banana skin is slippery because its squashed inside makes a slimy layer between your shoe and the floor.
exercise: Why does oil on a squeaky hinge stop the squeak? | It makes a slippery layer between the metal parts | It makes the hinge heavier | It fills the hinge with air | It makes the metal soft and bendy, so it moves quietly
exercise: Why do car engines need oil? | Without it the metal parts would rub, heat up and seize | Oil is what makes the car go | Oil keeps the engine cold in winter | Oil makes the car lighter
exercise: Why is a suitcase on wheels easier to move than dragging it? | Wheels roll instead of rub | Wheels make the case lighter | Wheels push the ground away | Wheels catch the wind
exercise: How did people move huge stones before wheels? | By rolling them on logs | By floating them on balloons | By sliding them on dry sand | By lifting them with ropes only
exercise: What are the little balls inside a skateboard wheel for? | They roll so the wheel hardly rubs on its axle | They make a nice rattling sound as the board rolls | They make the board heavier | They keep the wheel cool
exercise: Which of these also has ball bearings? | A washing machine | A sharpened pencil | A wooden mixing spoon | A fuzzy tennis ball
exercise: Why do climbers put chalk on their hands? | It soaks up sweat so they grip better | It makes their hands lighter | It keeps their hands warm | It shows other climbers where they have been
exercise: Why does a rubber glove help open a jar? | Soft rubber grips the lid better than smooth skin | Rubber somehow makes your whole hand much stronger | The glove warms the lid | Rubber makes the lid smaller
exercise: Why does hot water on a metal lid help open a jar? | The metal lid grows a little | The glass of the jar melts a little | The jam inside gets a bit lighter | Hot water is slippery like oil
exercise: Why can't you pull apart two books with interleaved pages? | The grips of hundreds of pages add up | The pages are glued together by the printer | Paper is magnetic | The books are too heavy to lift
exercise: Why does a spacecraft need a heat shield coming home? | It rushes in so fast that the squeezed air gets very hot | Space is hotter than the Sun | The rocket engine is underneath | It flies too close to the Moon
exercise: Why are fish so hard to hold? | Their slime is slippery like oil | They are always much too heavy to lift | They have no bones at all inside them | Their scales are a little magnetic
exercise: Why do tyres screech when they spin on a fast start? | A sliding tyre grips less than a rolling one | Tyres scream like that when they are still cold | The road is pushing the car back | The engine is too quiet
exercise: When does a ruler on a tilted desk start to slide? | When the slope is steep enough for gravity to beat friction | As soon as the desk tilts even a little | Only when someone pushes it | Never, rulers always stay put
exercise: Why does a rubber eraser stay put on a slope where a ruler slides? | Rubber has much more friction | The eraser is much heavier | Erasers are pulled less by gravity | The eraser is sticky with glue
exercise: Why do knots in your laces hold? | The lace rubs on itself | The lace is glued at the knot | The shoe holds the knot | Laces are magnetic
test: What would a day with no friction at all be like? | You could not walk, hold a pencil or tie your shoes | Everything would be easier to hold | Nothing would ever move | Things would only fall upwards
test: argh this jar won't open, any tips?? | Use a rubber glove or warm the lid | Put it in the freezer for an hour first | Shake it hard while holding it upside down | Oil the whole lid all over so it slides
test: My mate says a spacecraft glows only cos it rubs on the air. right? | Mostly the squeezed air in front heats up | Yes, it is only rubbing | No, its engines are what make it glow so brightly | No, the Sun heats it up
test: Why would a gymnast with sweaty hands be in danger on the bars? | Sweaty skin is slippery and she could slip | Sweat makes her too heavy | Sweat freezes onto the cold metal bars at once | Sweat makes the bar bend
test: In a cartoon a cat slides across the kitchen on a banana skin. Why is a banana skin slippery? | It puts a slippery layer under your foot | Bananas are always magnetic | The banana skin bounces you up into the air | Bananas are lighter than air
test: You must move a heavy fridge across a room. Which helps most? | Put it on a trolley with wheels | Push it hard from the top corner | Put a thick heavy rug on top of it | Push it much faster at the start
test: Why does a fisherman wet his hands before holding a fish? | So he does not rub off the slime that protects it | So the fish is easier to grip | Because dry hands are too cold | So the fish stays heavier
test: What would happen to a car engine with no oil? | Its parts would rub, overheat and seize | It would go faster | It would use less petrol | Nothing at all
test: wheels spinning and screaming, what should the driver do? | Start more smoothly so the tyres roll and grip | Press the pedal down even harder to get away quicker | Turn the steering wheel quickly | Put the brakes on and off
test: Why do skateboards spin for ages but an old cart wheel creaks to a stop? | Ball bearings roll instead of rub | Skateboards are pulled less by gravity | Cart wheels are heavier than skates | Old wood has no friction at all
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of lubricating oil in a hinge? | It interposes a slippery layer between the metal surfaces | It increases the mass of the moving parts | It renders the metal soft and pliable | It attracts the parts towards one another
exam: Identify the principal advantage of a wheel over a sledge for the conveyance of heavy loads. | Rolling occasions far less friction than sliding | A wheel diminishes the weight of the load considerably | A wheel eliminates the effect of gravity | Sliding occasions less friction than rolling
exam: It is given that two books are interleaved page by page. Ascertain the reason for which they resist separation. | The friction of numerous pages is cumulatively very great | The pages are united by an adhesive | The books exert a magnetic attraction | The air between the pages is removed entirely
exam: Determine the consequence for ordinary human activity of the total absence of friction. | Walking and grasping objects would be impossible | Walking would be rendered effortless | Objects would adhere to the hands | All bodies would remain permanently at rest upon the ground
